<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where's the Puddle? – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where's the Puddle?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Identify the main ideas and details in short narratives.<br>reading2: Respond creatively to stories read.<br>phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can locate key information about the weather in readings (e.g., 'It is rainy in June.').<br>Can respond creatively by identifying weather words in short stories (e.g., 'rain, wet, puddle.').<br>Can apply phonemic awareness and alphabet knowledge to read and spell one-syllable words related to weather (e.g., /sk/ /aɪ/ in 'sky,' /m/ /ʌdi/ in 'muddy').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large flashcards of core vocabulary words from Lesson 1: 'rain', 'puddle', 'umbrella', 'raincoat', 'boots', 'muddy'. The teacher points to each flashcard and asks, "What's this?" Students respond chorally. The teacher then plays a short audio clip of rain sounds and asks, "What do you hear?" to connect to the "Rainy Season" scenario.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the flashcard for 'puddle' and says, "Puddle. Puddle. Where's the puddle?" The teacher models the pronunciation of 'rain' emphasizing the /r/ sound, and 'puddle' emphasizing the /p/ sound. The teacher then models reading a simple sentence: "It is rainy. I need my umbrella." pointing to the corresponding flashcards.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'umbrella' flashcard) "Is this a coat?" (Expected: No!)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'boots' flashcard) "Do we wear boots when it is sunny?" (Expected: No!)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> Students will read a short, illustrated narrative titled "Sammy's Rainy Day". The story describes Sammy's experience on a rainy day, explicitly using target vocabulary (rain, puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, muddy, wet, splash, sky, cloud) and simple present/present continuous sentences (e.g., "It is raining now. Sammy wears his raincoat and boots. He loves to splash in the muddy puddles."). The text will be projected or provided as a handout.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short illustrated narrative excerpt (e.g., a simple story on a large chart or individual printed sheets with pictures).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding short, illustrated stories about daily activities and common weather conditions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (after initial silent reading and teacher reading aloud):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the story about? A sunny day or a rainy day?" Students point to a sun or rain picture.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads sentences, and students respond 'True' or 'False' by showing thumbs up/down. For example: "Sammy wears sandals in the rain." "Sammy splashes in a puddle."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students read a word from the story (e.g., 'umbrella') and match it to the corresponding picture.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying main ideas and details, locating key information, and applying phonemic awareness to read one-syllable words. Grammar: Present simple/continuous for description. Vocabulary: puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, muddy, wet, splash, rain.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/Cloze Reading:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from "Sammy's Rainy Day" with key vocabulary words missing. Below each sentence, a choice of two words (one correct, one incorrect) is provided. Students read the sentence and choose the correct word to fill in the blank. Example: "It is __________. (rainy / sunny) Sammy needs his ___________. (sandals / boots)"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Reading for Sounds:</b> The teacher writes a few target words from the story on the board (e.g., 'rain', 'puddle', 'splash', 'drop', 'muddy'). Students read each word aloud. The teacher asks students to identify words with the /r/ sound and the /p/ sound. The teacher then guides students to blend sounds to read words like /sk/ /aɪ/ 'sky' or /m/ /ʌdi/ 'muddy' from a list of words related to the story, reinforcing phonemic awareness.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Reading & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students re-read specific sections of "Sammy's Rainy Day" (or a simplified version provided). They then ask each other simple WH-questions about the text, (e.g., "What does Sammy wear?" "Where does Sammy splash?"). Students point to the answer in the text. This reinforces reading for details and builds confidence in reading comprehension.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Where's My Rainy Gear?" Reading Challenge.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students receive a worksheet with 3-4 short descriptive sentences about different rainy season items (e.g., "I am big and round. I keep you dry. You hold me in your hand. What am I?"). Next to each description, there are three small pictures: one correct and two distractors (e.g., umbrella, raincoat, boots). Students read each description independently and circle the correct picture. This directly connects to the theme "Where's the Puddle?" by focusing on items needed in the rain.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension for specific details, connecting text to visuals, and demonstrating understanding of target vocabulary (umbrella, raincoat, boots, puddle, storm) and adjectives (wet, rainy).</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align="justify" style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> The teacher will observe students during the "Where's My Rainy Gear?" reading challenge, noting their ability to read the descriptions and correctly identify items. The completed worksheet will also serve as a formal assessment of reading comprehension and vocabulary recognition.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will complete the "Where's My Rainy Gear?" worksheet (as described in Stage 4). Additionally, they will identify two words from the descriptions that contain the /r/ sound (e.g., 'rain', 'round') and one word with the /p/ sound (e.g., 'splash', 'puddle' if included in descriptions), writing them down next to the activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately identifies pictures based on short text descriptions (locates key information).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of target vocabulary (e.g., 'umbrella', 'raincoat').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify words with target phonemes /r/ and /p/.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completes the task independently (responds creatively by circling).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by circling "Yes" or "No" to the following:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about rainy weather things."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the words 'rain' and 'puddle' when I read."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new rainy day words did you learn to read today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when reading the story or descriptions?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the reading activities? Were the texts and tasks appropriately challenging for 4th graders at A1.2? Did most students successfully identify main ideas and details? Were there any specific vocabulary words or grammatical structures that proved difficult to understand in written form? How effectively did the phonemic awareness practice support reading? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students struggling with reading comprehension or identifying target sounds?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we focused on reading about rainy weather and what we need. Next lesson, we will practice speaking and asking questions about our favorite rainy day activities and what we wear, using the words we learned today!"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>